> >
> > Sometimes I forget the basics, especially when working in these higher-level frameworks. This is very close to what we are trying to infer. However, If we calculated the probability of two proteins coming from the same complex, how would we combine this information to assign multiple memberships per protein?
>
> The model implicitly assumes every protein comes from a single
> mixture component.  If you want to relax this, you need to go to
> some kind of mixed-membership model like LDA.
>
> > Re-reading the formulation I gave in the prior email, I think I oversimplified it a bit. The co-migration is not restricted to two proteins, it can be anywhere between 2-50 proteins. So I guess the probability of interest is something in the line of,
> >
> >  Pr[n in same cluster as n’, n’’, … | y]
> >
> > can this be calculated downstream from the Pr[n in same cluster as n’| y]? Calculating it for all given combinations seems unfeasible.
>
> Yes, anything combinatoric like this is intractable.
> And there's no way of getting around that no matter what you
> do.  People often try to fudge this with something like k-means
> or LDA, but those are so highly multimodal in their posteriors,
> you can't actually do the proper inference.  Every initial point
> leads to a different local maximum and different inferences.
>
> > Digging deeper in the google scholar pages, I actually stumped on this article http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sguennem/publications/ICDM2013a.pdf. It describes a mixed membership mixture model (I am assuming it is a legal copy, since it comes from the main authors web site.)
>
> That's usually not a good assumption legally.  Computer
> scientists are notorious IP pirates as a rule.
>
> > Of course, this does not capture the generative process exactly, as each of the places are physically connected sequentially (i.e. 1-2-3-4-5, …). But practically, that might be OK. In theory, I should be able to implement the model in Stan using the same summarisation as for a normal mixture model, right?
>
> I'm afraid I'm really not going to have time to do the background
> reading here.
>
> > > Then I'd probably try to add some multivariate structure to the clustering,
> > > but first things first.
> > >
> > > And I don't think you'll need 1000 clusters --- how many clusters do you think
> > > are actually operating?
> >
> > If each of the clusters represent a physical complex (a set of proteins), and assuming each protein can be a member of multiple clusters, 1,000 is not an unreasonable high number.
>
> Then you're probably going to need some customized approximate
> software to stand a chance at this problem.  The combinatorics
> will be well beyond what you can apply full Bayesian inference to.
>
> - Bob
>

