rstan already generates the C++, but puts it in an R tmp directory (wherever that is for your system) so you could grab the C++ from there.  You'll need to orchestrate the compilation but you could use rstan as a template to figure out how to do that (as reliably as rstan does it).  Another alternative for generating the C++ is to use CmdStan which generates an executable (stanc?) that does this .stan -> .cpp step.  That's the gist of it, but you're probably right that figuring out what rstanarm does is the best way to go first. Krzysztof

