> Also, what would actually be the proper way to express this in maths? Reverse engineering your expression I come up with,
>
> p(y | theta, mu, sigma, beta)_t = \sum_i^K theta_i * N(y_t | mu+beta[k]*y_{t-1}, sigma) for t = 2 .. T

That looks OK, but I'd put the _t on y, not on p().  And then there's a product
on the outside

  p(y[2:T] | theta, mu, sigma, beta)

    = PROD_{t in 2:T} SUM_{k in 1:K} (theta[k] * N(y[t] | mu + beta[k] * y[t-1], sigma)

And then you can add in some likelihood for y[1] if you want but it can't depend
on y[0] because there won't be one.

