Yes, I think that's exactly what's going on.  And I think that's
what happens in the Dirichlet normalization to let it operate over
simplexes (K dimensional simplex involves only K-1 dimensions
underlyingly).

I think if you declare the appropriate constraints on your x
parameters (or transform them there from raw), then you can just
put the regular generative density on x and everything will work out.
You should try that with simulated data to confirm

BUT (and this is a biggy), my understanding of the intricacies of
conditional densities and the geometry of lower-dimensional submanifolds
is sketchy at best.

I still never understood why constraining a vector to be ordered,
then giving each component a normal gives you the same distribution
as giving a bunch of unconstrained vector components normal priors
and then sorting,

parameteters
   ordered_vector[4] x;
model
   x ~ normal(0, 1);

VS

parameters
  vector[4] x_raw;
transformed parameters
  ordered_vector[4] x;
  x <- sort(x_raw);
model
  x_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

- Bob

