Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2015 04:06:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Re: Dynamically sized types in user defined functions?
Message-ID: <nhm9RnxN8Zg.aZ4TeWgcBAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <nhm9RnxN8Zg.7RGspqX5AwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <nhm9RnxN8Zg.RZVNeiHjAwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <nhm9RnxN8Zg.7RGspqX5AwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/nhm9RnxN8Zg/m/aZ4TeWgcBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two ways around that.

1.  Put the sizing in a function and call it:

  vector[my_size_function(a,b,c)] x;

2.  Put it in a local variable:

  int k;
  k <- 1;

  ...
  {
     vector[k] x;
     ... use x ...
  }
  ... can't use x ...

Usually these integer floor and ceiling functions are a bad idea,
which is why we haven't built them in.  The exception is in interpolation,
where we'd like to have some built-in function support.

- Bob

