Even when you do have strong information, as Geoff may have, it is sometimes strong information on an expression which involves data and parameters together (as diff does here) and that is still information we can use in a legitimate way (that is, in a way which has a meaningful interpretation and which helps define a bayesian posterior even in the absence of an iid sampling story). In this particular case, in the presence of y the x values are not independent, but we can still say that plausible values for "diff" are given by the high probability region of a lognormal.

