> The only time I have strong information about how my data was generated
> is when I simulate said data myself for testing some code.
>
> I don't know about other fields, but in social sciences such notions of
> "strong information" about the DGP are rapidly shipwrecked by the
> laughter of the gods.

Strong is relative here.  In physics it can be extremely detailed, for
example the transition from incident particle to EM shower to photons to
photodetector current to op amp voltage to readout.  Social sciences
may not be anywhere near as detailed, but considering a generative
narrative for, say, poll non-response or population exchangeability is
still the best way to identify a robust likelihood.

I’ll side eye any analysis claiming a generative model from quantum
mechanics to neuronal activity to human decision to poll response
too, but I’ll also suspect any analysis that uses a default likelihood
with no further consideration.

