From ufc5d3270037d7478 at groups.google.com  Thu Sep 24 23:15:53 2015
From: ufc5d3270037d7478 at groups.google.com (Jonah)
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2015 23:15:53 +0000
Subject: fitting binomial nonlinear model in Stan question
Message-ID: <-A4G6N7wqAo.XZJxjp0yBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <-A4G6N7wqAo.uZd2V4skBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <-A4G6N7wqAo.uZd2V4skBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/-A4G6N7wqAo/m/XZJxjp0yBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Several things here:

(1) y and n should be declared as integer array and not vectors (which contain real numbers). So you need int y[N] and likewise for n.

(2) pi should be declared in the transformed parameters block since it is a deterministic function of parameters. It also needs to have N elements, one for each value of conc. The statement assigning a value to pi that you have in the model block also should move to transformed parameters as a loop (I don't think exp is vectorized yet, but I might be wrong).

That should get the model running. However, you can and should avoid all of (2) by using y~binomial_logit(n, beta[1]*(conc - beta[2])), which does it all on the logit scale for you so you don't need to do the transformation yourself.

I hope that helps. If any of that doesn't make sense let me know.

From ufc5d3270037d7478 at groups.google.com  Thu Sep 24 23:27:29 2015
From: ufc5d3270037d7478 at groups.google.com (Jonah)
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2015 23:27:29 +0000
Subject: Re: fitting binomial nonlinear model in Stan question
Message-ID: <-A4G6N7wqAo.iel8nj8zBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <-A4G6N7wqAo.XZJxjp0yBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <-A4G6N7wqAo.uZd2V4skBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <-A4G6N7wqAo.XZJxjp0yBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/-A4G6N7wqAo/m/iel8nj8zBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There's also an inverse logit function (inv_logit) which you can use instead of doing all the exping yourself. But the recommended way is to just use binomial_logit so that you get to work on the linear scale and Stan takes care of the nonlinear transformation for you (with greater numerical stability too).

