From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Sep 28 17:29:22 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2015 17:29:22 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Poisson model running slowly
Message-ID: <yjt2zpA67BM.L3se6QZaBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <yjt2zpA67BM.taS8iLZTBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <yjt2zpA67BM.taS8iLZTBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/yjt2zpA67BM/m/L3se6QZaBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Declare your covariates as a matrix

  matrix[N,M] x;

That is, x[n,1] = drug[n], ...  And declare your
coeffs as a vector

  vector[M] beta;

with intercept

  real alpha;

and then code your model as

  y ~ poisson_log(x * beta + intercept + alpha);

This should make it a bit faster per iteration, but won't
change the posterior.  I'm a bit confused by the naming, because
you have a constant precomputed intercept --- is that just an
offset by the data mean of y?  If so, you could calculate that
in the model as transformed data and it'd be more self-contained
and reliable (no chance of error passing in mismatched data).

What you may need to do is provide some kind of prior on
the coefficients alpha and beta.

- Bob

